---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import CompareSlider from '../../../components/CompareSlider.astro';
import ScreenshotLightbox from '../../../components/ScreenshotLightbox.astro';
import { localizedShot } from '../../../data/projects';
import '../../../styles/case-study.css';

const lang = 'en';

const shots = [
  '/screenshots/local-sites-01-home-split.webp?v=2',
  '/screenshots/local-sites-02-why-us-split.webp?v=2',
  '/screenshots/local-sites-03-services-split.webp?v=2',
  '/screenshots/local-sites-04-mobile-both.webp?v=2',
].map((src) => localizedShot(src, lang));

const delivered = [
  { title: 'Domains and hosting', body: 'Registered and set up for both branches, so each has its own address.' },
  { title: 'One WordPress build', body: 'Home, services, about and contact pages, with a detail page for every service each branch offers.' },
  { title: 'Two brand themes', body: 'Each branch styled from its own logo and brand guidelines: colours, type and personality.' },
  { title: 'Content and photos', body: 'Their own photos, topped up with a few stock images where a service needed one.' },
  { title: 'Ways to get in touch', body: 'A spam-protected contact form, phone, email and opening hours on every page, and their social profiles linked in.' },
  { title: 'Mobile first', body: 'Built for phones first, since that’s where most people look for a workshop.' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  lang={lang}
  title="EBB & Baja Garage — case study"
  description="Two websites for two branches of the same auto repair business: one shared site system, styled as two distinct brands."
>
  <article class="container-page py-20 sm:py-28">
    <a href="/en/projects/#auto-shop" class="text-sm font-medium text-fg/50 hover:text-fg">← All projects</a>
    <p class="mt-8 text-sm font-medium text-accent-light">Case study · Client work</p>
    <h1 class="mt-4 max-w-3xl font-display text-4xl font-medium tracking-tight text-fg sm:text-5xl">
      EBB &amp; Baja Garage: one family, two personalities
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl text-lg text-fg/60">
      Two websites for two branches of the same auto repair business, built so they feel like
      the same family while each keeps its own character.
    </p>

    <dl class="mt-10 grid max-w-3xl gap-6 border-y border-fg/10 py-6 text-sm sm:grid-cols-3">
      <div><dt class="text-fg/40">Role</dt><dd class="mt-1 text-fg">Websites, hosting and domains</dd></div>
      <div><dt class="text-fg/40">Built with</dt><dd class="mt-1 text-fg">WordPress, local SEO</dd></div>
      <div>
        <dt class="text-fg/40">Live sites</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 text-fg">
          <a href="https://ebb.com.mx/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-accent-light hover:underline">ebb.com.mx</a><br />
          <a href="https://bajagarage.com.mx/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-accent-light hover:underline">bajagarage.com.mx</a>
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <div class="relative mt-12 aspect-[8/5] overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-fg/10">
      <CompareSlider
        before={localizedShot('/screenshots/local-sites-01-home-ebb.webp?v=2', lang)}
        after={localizedShot('/screenshots/local-sites-01-home-baja.webp?v=2', lang)}
        labels={['EBB', 'Baja Garage']}
        alt="Homepage"
        ariaLabel="Drag to compare EBB and Baja Garage"
      />
      <button type="button" data-lightbox-trigger data-images={JSON.stringify(shots)} data-index="0" class="absolute right-3 top-3 z-10 rounded-full bg-surface/90 px-2.5 py-1 text-xs font-medium text-fg transition-colors hover:bg-fg hover:text-bg">
        View {shots.length} photos
      </button>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-fg/40">Drag the handle to switch between the two brands.</p>

    <div class="prose-case mt-16 grid gap-16">
      <section>
        <h2>The brief</h2>
        <p>
          One owner, two workshops: EBB in Guanajuato and Baja Garage in Aguascalientes. Neither
          branch had a website, and they were setting up their social media at the same time, so
          the sites had to be the professional home that everything else points back to.
        </p>
        <p>
          The ask was specific. They wanted both sites to look the same, so customers would feel
          it's the same company, the same family, but with each branch keeping its own
          personality.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>What I delivered</h2>
        <p>
          They supplied their logos, brand guidelines and photos. I handled everything else, from
          domains and hosting through to launch:
        </p>
        <ul class="not-prose mt-6 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
          {delivered.map((d) => (
            <li class="rounded-xl border border-fg/10 bg-surface p-4">
              <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-fg">{d.title}</p>
              <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-fg/55">{d.body}</p>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>One system, two brands</h2>
        <p>
          Rather than building two separate websites, I built one site system and styled it twice.
          The structure, page layouts and content model are shared; the logo, colours, typefaces and
          details are each branch's own. EBB is cool navy with clean, light type; Baja Garage is
          bold red with a sharper, more technical feel.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Consistency for free.</strong> Both sites behave the same way, so the business feels like one family wherever a customer lands.</li>
          <li><strong>Each branch still stands on its own.</strong> Its own domain, phone number, hours, city and services. Baja Garage offers bodywork and paint, so its site has a page EBB's doesn't.</li>
          <li><strong>Cheaper to build and maintain.</strong> Work done once, like a new page layout or a fix, can be carried across to the other site instead of being rebuilt.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>The trap with twin sites</h2>
        <p>
          Sharing a system has one classic risk: text written for one branch quietly ends up on the
          other. While putting this case study together I found exactly that, a line on Baja
          Garage's homepage that still named EBB's city. It was fixed the same day.
        </p>
        <p>
          It's a small thing, but it's what a customer notices. The lesson: with twin sites, every
          change needs checking on both for anything that belongs to only one of them.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Results</h2>
        <p>
          About 60 hours of work took the business from no web presence to two live, mobile-first
          websites, each with its own domain, a full set of service pages and a direct way for
          customers to get in touch. Every social profile now has a proper home to point to.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="rounded-2xl border border-fg/10 bg-surface p-8">
        <h2 class="!mt-0">Running a local business?</h2>
        <p>
          You don't need anything flashy, just a site that's fast, clear, easy to update and makes
          it simple for customers to reach you. If you have more than one location, it can share
          the work and still feel like each one's own.
        </p>
        <a href="/en/contact/" class="not-prose mt-4 inline-block rounded-full bg-fg px-5 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-bg transition-transform hover:scale-[1.02]">Start the conversation</a>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>

  <ScreenshotLightbox />
</BaseLayout>
